  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current optimization method based on continuous function representations suffers from slow convergence and is prone to local optima due to its reliance on gradient-based techniques and linear interpolation. This improved approach embraces sparsity and discontinuities inspired by known near-optimal solutions for the Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem. The key innovations are:\n1) A new sparse representation using M Gaussian pulses, allowing us to control overlap independence\n2) A fitness function that computes exact overlap using line integrals \n3) An evolutionary optimization with guided crossover to avoid local minima\n4) Dynamic control over pulse width and amplitude parameters\n\nThis method leverages mathematical insights from harmonic analysis and discrepancy theory, parameterizing the solution in terms of pulse parameters rather than direct grid values. The lower-is-better optimization maintains symmetry and effectively reduces the degrees of freedom, making the problem more tractable.\n\nMathematical specification:\nDefine pulse function with:\n- Centers: c \u2208 \u211d\u1d39, c_i \u2208 [0,2]\n- Widths: w \u2208 \u211d\u1d39\u207a, w_i = \u03c3_w / i^{\u03b2_w}\n- Amplitudes: a \u2208 [0,1]\u1d39\n\nThe function h(x) is defined as:\nh(x) = \u2211\u1d62 (a\u1d62 / (\u03c3_a \u221a(2\u03c0))) * exp(-(x-c\u1d62)\u00b2/(2\u03c3_a\u00b2))\n\nThe constraints are:\n1. \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n2. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n3. The overlap integral: max\u2096\u208c\u208b\u221e^\u221e \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nThe cost is optimized over M pulses with exponentially tapered widths to ensure mathematical continuity.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize pulse parameters: M values for centers, a, b\n2. Compute grid function by discretizing h(x)\n3. Project to satisfy usage constraints and evaluate cost\n4. Optimization loop (200,000 iterations) blending:\n   - Local search adjusting pulse parameters\n   - Evolutionary crossover guided by gradient information\n   - Use of Chebyshev nodes for candidate selection\n\nKey cost function variants:\n- Base: Gaussian profile\n- Alternate: Lorentzian profile for better overlap properties\n- Stress-testing with extreme parameter ratios\n\nThe optimization dynamically adjusts pulse width exponent \u03b2_w and amplitude scaling \u03c3_a to induce candidate diversity. Initial pulse placements are randomized with adaptive clustering to maintain coverage.\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Pulse count M: Default 4 with stress-testing of M=3,5,6\n- Width scaling \u03b2_w: Default 0.3 with adaptive tuning\n- Amplitude scaling \u03c3_a: Default 0.5145, stress-tested at 0.4115\n- Debiasing: \u03c4 parameter for asymmetry control\n\nCut-off response for plateaus triggers restarts. Abnormal optimization paths use fallback to known sparse solutions.\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Wall-clock: ~650s for gradient optimization\n- Fallback: Poisson-disc sampling pattern if optimization stalls\n- Repair: Bounded variation filtering for lost solutions\n- Near-optimal: Candidate repair if integral constraint violated\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. \u222b h(x) dx \u2248 1 within 1e-3 error\n2. 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1, with \u2211h = n/2 exactly\n3. Cost computed via trapezoidal rule\n4. Nested fallback ensures legal solution\n5. Final output clipped to [0,1]",
